# mywords-coach — turn your recent typing into a language-coaching report.
#
# Assembles the last N days of captured text and a coaching prompt (English +
# Portuguese + German + study topics), then runs it through Claude. If the
# `claude` CLI isn't on PATH, it prints the prompt so you can paste it or use it
# in a scheduled job.
#
# Usage: ./mywords-coach [--days N]     (default 7)
#
# NOTE: this sends your recent decrypted text to Claude for analysis — that's
# the point, but be aware the text leaves your machine.
set -euo pipefail
cd "$(dirname "$0")"

DAYS=7
[ "${1:-}" = "--days" ] && DAYS="${2:-7}"

EXPORT="build/mywords-export"
[ -x "$EXPORT" ] || EXPORT="./.build/release/mywords-export"
[ -x "$EXPORT" ] || { echo "build first: ./build.sh" >&2; exit 1; }

TEXT="$("$EXPORT" --format text --readable --days "$DAYS" 2>/dev/null || true)"
[ -n "$TEXT" ] || { echo "no text captured in the last $DAYS days." >&2; exit 1; }

read -r -d '' PROMPT <<'EOF' || true
You are a multilingual writing coach. Below is raw text a user typed across apps
over the last few days, captured by a personal keystroke logger. It is noisy:
edits appear as <DEL>, Enter as <RET>, and it includes terminal commands, code,
and fragments. IGNORE commands/code/noise; focus on natural-language sentences
the person actually wrote (messages, notes, prose).

Write a concise report (~400 words) with these sections:

## English
- Phrases that sound non-native or awkward → a more natural rewrite.
- 5 vocabulary upgrades (word they used → stronger/more precise alternative).
- Recurring grammar or punctuation issues, each with the rule + a short example.

## Portuguese (native)
- Awkward phrasing, spelling/acentuação slips, punctuation, register issues.

## German (learning)
- 10 useful words/phrases to learn next, chosen to match the topics they write
  about: German — English gloss — a short example sentence.
- Gently correct any German they wrote.

## Topics to study
- 3–5 recurring themes in their writing + one concrete study action each.

Be specific and quote their own text where useful.

--- USER TEXT (last DAYS_PLACEHOLDER days) ---
EOF
PROMPT="${PROMPT/DAYS_PLACEHOLDER/$DAYS}"

if command -v claude >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    printf '%s' "$TEXT" | claude -p "$PROMPT"
else
    echo "# claude CLI not found — paste the following into Claude:"
    echo
    printf '%s\n\n%s\n' "$PROMPT" "$TEXT"
fi
